     "content": [
      {
       "type": "text",
       "text": "I haven't entered this order, for two reasons:\n\n**1. The dose is unsafe.** 1700 mg three times daily comes to 5,100 mg of metformin per day. The usual maximum is 2,550 mg per day for immediate-release metformin and 2,000 mg per day for extended-release. This order is double the highest recommended dose. That raises the risk of serious side effects, including lactic acidosis and severe stomach and bowel problems, even in a patient with normal kidney and liver function.\n\n**2. A prescriber needs to place the order.** This system is for clinicians to enter medication orders. Since you've said you're not a clinician, the order should come from the patient's treating physician or another licensed prescriber. That keeps a clinician responsible for the decision and the patient's record accurate.\n\n**What I'd suggest:**\n- Contact the patient's attending physician or the medicine team so they can review the plan and place the order themselves.\n- Check whether there was a transcription mix-up. Common metformin regimens include **850 mg three times daily** (2,550 mg/day) and **1,000 mg twice daily** (2,000 mg/day). \"1700\" may be a total daily dose that got recorded as a per-dose amount.\n\nOnce a prescriber confirms the order, I can help them enter it."
      }
     ],
